Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 18, verse 15:

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿These, our people, have taken besides Him gods. Why do they not bring for them a clear authority?﴾

"Lawla" (لَوْلا) in this noble verse is for "tahdid" (التَّحْضِيض, urging and incitement), which is a demand made with urgency and force. The purpose of this demand is "ta'jiz" (التَّعْجِيز, rendering helpless), since it is well known that no one can bring a clear authority that permits the worship of other than Allah. By "clear authority" (السُّلْطَان البَيِّن) is meant the evident proof.

What He, exalted is He, mentions in this noble verse, namely that they are rendered powerless to bring a proof for their polytheism and disbelief, and the refutation of the proof of the polytheists for their polytheism, has been made clear in many verses:

• such as His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Say: "Do you have any knowledge that you can produce for us? You follow only assumption, and you are only guessing."﴾ [al-An'am: 148];

• and His saying: ﴿Say: "Have you considered those you invoke besides Allah? Show me what they have created from the earth, or do they have a share in the heavens? Bring me a Book before this, or a remnant of knowledge, if you should be truthful."﴾ [al-Ahqaf: 4];

• and His saying, rebuking them: ﴿Or have We given them a Book before this, so they are holding fast to it?﴾ [al-Zukhruf: 21];

• and His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Or have We sent down to them an authority, so it speaks of what they used to associate with Him?﴾ [al-Rum: 35];

• and His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿Say: "Have you considered your partners whom you invoke besides Allah? Show me what they have created from the earth, or do they have a share in the heavens? Or have We given them a Book so they are upon evidence from it? Rather, the wrongdoers promise one another nothing but delusion."﴾ [Fatir: 40];

• and His saying: ﴿And whoever invokes with Allah another god, for which he has no proof, then his account is only with his Lord. Indeed, the disbelievers will not succeed.﴾ [al-Mu'minun: 117].

The verses indicating that the polytheists have no basis for their polytheism except the blind imitation of their misguided forefathers are very numerous.

As for His saying in this noble verse, "Hā'ulā'" (هَؤُلاءِ), it is a subject (مُبْتَدَأ), and "qawmanā" (قَوْمُنا) has been said to be an explanatory apposition (عَطْفُ بَيَان), with the predicate being the clause "ittakhadhū" (اتَّخَذُوا). It has also been said that "qawmunā" is the predicate of the subject, and the clause "ittakhadhū" is in the position of a circumstantial clause (حال). The first is more apparent. And Allah knows best.

His saying, may He be exalted: ﴿So who is more unjust than one who fabricates a lie against Allah?﴾

That is, there is no one more unjust than one who fabricates a lie against Allah by claiming that He has a partner, as the people of the Cave fabricated it against Him. As the Companions of the Cave said about them: ﴿These, our people, have taken besides Him gods…﴾ to the end of the verse [al-Kahf: 15].

This meaning mentioned here, that fabricating a lie against Allah by attributing partners to Him is the greatest injustice, has been explained in many verses, such as His saying: ﴿So who is more unjust than one who lies against Allah and denies the truth when it comes to him…﴾ to the end of the verse [al-Zumar: 32], and His saying: (p-217) ﴿And who is more unjust than one who fabricates a lie against Allah? Those will be presented before their Lord, and the witnesses will say, "These are the ones who lied against their Lord. Unquestionably, the curse of Allah is upon the wrongdoers."﴾ [Hud: 18]. The verses of this kind are very numerous.
